  <bioghist id="15948dded131ee4f8e061f7acfe9d67d">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Ronald T. Gault's career encompassed jobs in civil rights work, government, philanthropy, and investment banking. From 1969 to 1978, he worked at the Ford Foundation as a Program Officer in the Division of National Affairs, Social Development Program.</p><p>After leaving the Ford Foundation, Gault became a senior advisor to New York City Mayor Ed Koch. He then became a Vice President at First Boston Corp. He later moved to Johannesburg, South Africa where he worked as CEO of business development and client relations for JP Morgan.</p><p>Gault earned a B.A. from Grinnell College and a master's degree from the University of Michigan. Prior to joining the Ford Foundation, Gault had jobs at the Urban League and later the Community Relations Service (CRS) within the United States Department of Justice. He is married to journalist and civil rights activist Charlayne Hunter-Gault who formerly served as CNN's Johannesburg bureau chief.</p>  </bioghist>
